The complex number $z$ can be represented as $z = 2e^{i\theta}$, where $\theta$ is the angle in radians. We need to analyze the expression $z + \frac{1}{z}$ to understand the path traced by $z + \frac{1}{z}$ as $\theta$ varies from $0$ to $2\pi$. 

\begin{align*} z &= 2e^{i\theta} = 2(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta) \\ \frac{1}{z} &= \frac{1}{2e^{i\theta}} = \frac{1}{2}(\cos(-\theta) + i\sin(-\theta)) = \frac{1}{2}(\cos\theta - i\sin\theta) \\ \text{So, } z + \frac{1}{z} &= 2(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta) + \frac{1}{2}(\cos\theta - i\sin\theta) \\ &= \left(\frac{5}{2}\cos\theta + \frac{3}{2}\cos\theta\right) + i\left(\frac{5}{2}\sin\theta - \frac{3}{2}\sin\theta\right) \\ &= \left(\frac{5}{2}\cos\theta + \frac{3}{2}\cos\theta\right) + i\left(\frac{5}{2}\sin\theta - \frac{3}{2}\sin\theta\right) \\ &= \left(\frac{5}{2} + \frac{3}{2}\right)\cos\theta + i\left(\frac{5}{2} - \frac{3}{2}\right)\sin\theta \\ &= 4\cos\theta + 2i\sin\theta \end{align*}

Let $x = 4\cos\theta$ and $y = 2\sin\theta$. Then we have $x/4 + y/(2i) = 1 \implies x^2/16 - y^2/4 = 1$ which is the equation of a hyperbola. <answer>$D$</answer>

